+
+**PTYs are available; lifetime and policy are the expensive parts.** Bionic implements `openpty`/`forkpty`, controlling-terminal setup and terminal size handling. Qualification must cover SIGINT/SIGHUP, EOF, resize, UTF-8/TUI parsing, detached-controller reconnection, inherited descriptors and log backpressure. These are proposed validation cases derived from the session contract, not observed Android defects. A separate app-server path needs equivalent connection and restart checks. [PTY] [SH]
+
+### Availability, networking, servers, and updates
+
+Ordinary Android apps face background execution restrictions and Doze. A foreground service is not an unlimited daemon exemption: for apps targeting Android 15 or later, the documented `dataSync` and `mediaProcessing` types each have a six-hour background budget per 24 hours. That limit does not apply identically to every service type. Native image-owned `init` daemons have a different lifecycle; it would be incorrect to apply the APK rule to all AOSP services. Both designs must handle memory pressure and process death. [ISOLATED] [DOZE] [FGS] [INIT] [LMKD]
+
+Network profiles need more than accounting by UID. The pinned Android eBPF program classifies sockets and applies specific UID policies; some paths exempt system identities, and background matching excludes loopback. The Swarma adapter must prove its own default-deny behavior across IPv4/IPv6, TCP/UDP including QUIC, DNS, local services, preexisting connections and delegated descriptors, with broker leases bound to trusted applied-policy evidence. Domain/URL policy still belongs in a trusted proxy. Do not let two owners independently overwrite the same netd/BPF state. [NETD] [CB] [B03]
+
+For stock-phone nodes, a user-approved VPN can apply per-app routing and an always-on/lockdown configuration, but there is only one active VPN service per user/profile. It is not equivalent to unrestricted platform firewall control and can conflict with the user's existing VPN. GrapheneOS's network permission adds a useful coarse control, not the complete destination profile. Treat these as explicitly different assurance profiles. [VPN] [GRAPHENE-FEATURES] [CB]
+
+An SSH server is feasible through an Android port, but the full remote-workplace design needs separate execution identities, constrained certificates/forced commands, forbidden forwarding and independent bridge credentials. A stock single-UID SSH app is not that architecture. Matrix **client** carriage is much cheaper than hosting a homeserver, database, backups, TLS ingress and restart policy on Android. Synapse documents Python plus native build dependencies and recommends PostgreSQL for substantial deployments; there is no Android qualification in the inspected install guide. These are additional porting/operations costs, not evidence that either server is mathematically impossible. [TERMUX-SSH] [B11] [SYNAPSE]
+
+Verified boot, signed updates and A/B recovery improve resistance to persistent system modification and failed OS deployment. They do not provide high availability, retain live PTYs through a reboot, replicate the board, or roll back mutable account generations and revocations safely. Swarma needs compatible data migrations, backups, explicit session recovery and durable closed/update-lock fences even with a successful boot fallback. Anti-rollback policy can intentionally forbid returning to a vulnerable OS; “rollback available” must not mean arbitrary downgrade. [AVB] [AB] [VAB] [SH] [UM]
+
+APEX supports versioned native/system components, including staged activation. Rebootless activation has conditions, including absence of use of the old package; it is not a general promise of hot replacement while runners hold old libraries. Mainline updates selected modules through supported delivery arrangements; a custom AOSP distribution still owns its custom components and its platform/vendor update path. Operator-only update authorization must cover those paths and their signing keys. [APEX] [MAINLINE] [ASB]
