+| Area | Cost / unresolved qualification |
+| --- | --- |
+| x86_64 and ARM64 | AOSP documents Cuttlefish targets for both architectures. This establishes virtual-device support, not arbitrary server-board support, network/storage drivers, accelerator support, bootloader locking, or a working hardware KeyMint HAL. Qualify a particular server platform before assigning hardware-backed guarantees. [CUTTLEFISH] [KEYSTORE] [AVB] |
+| Hardware trust | A generic server TPM is not automatically Android StrongBox. The selected hardware, HAL and attestation trust chain must implement the advertised security level. This audit establishes no supported physical server configuration with that complete chain. [KEYMINT] [ATTEST] |
+| Full AOSP build infrastructure | Current AOSP requirements specify an x86_64 Linux build host, at least 400 GB free disk and 64 GB RAM; current docs discuss newer branches than the pinned Android 16 code. These are full-build requirements, not runtime RAM requirements. Prebuilt Cuttlefish is the cheaper first probe. [BUILD] [CUTTLEFISH] |
+| Platform integration | Own the device configuration, service definitions, allocator, SELinux/seccomp policy, Keystore access, networking, storage/unlock model, installer and update packaging. Native services do not inherit all app sandbox behavior automatically. This is high initial engineering cost relative to adapting the existing Linux-oriented helper. [INIT] [SELINUX] [KEYSTORE] [UM] |
+| Recurring security maintenance | Track platform, Linux kernel, vendor/SoC, firmware, harness and toolchain updates; rebuild, sign and qualify images. The bulletin lists distinct upstream sources, including manufacturer-supplied fixes. Mainline is not a replacement for this work. [ASB] [MAINLINE] |
+| Qualification and recovery | Repeat policy, launcher, UID reuse, key, egress, PTY, upgrade and boot-recovery checks after relevant changes. Keep a physical-device lane for hardware claims and a virtual-device lane for logic. Cost is not credibly estimable in engineer-weeks without hardware selection and a compatibility probe. [SH] [UM] [ATTEST] [CUTTLEFISH] |
+
+## Comparison on the same guarantees
+
+This is a capability comparison, not a ranking by distribution name. “Custom” means a deployment must supply and test the property. None of the cited distributions implements Swarma grants, generations, input fences or durable delivery receipts. A strong Linux profile can combine existing kernel and deployment mechanisms while preserving the current userspace model; that is the basis for preferring it, not a claim that default Linux is equally hardened. [UM] [CB] [SH] [SYSTEMD] [FCOS-SELINUX]
+
+| Base / inspected source version | UID isolation, sandbox, per-agent egress | Boot trust / hardware keys | Updates and recovery | Swarma fit and cost judgment |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Custom AOSP, Android 16 reference | App UID + SELinux + seccomp available; native allocator and profile proxy custom | AVB and KeyMint integration; actual TEE/StrongBox depends on hardware; deterministic hierarchy gap | Signed A/B/Virtual A/B and APEX; mutable Swarma state remains ours | **High integration and recurring port cost.** Most attractive only as a dedicated appliance with a chosen hardware/security stack. [AID] [SANDBOX] [KEYMINT] [AVB] [AB] |
+| GrapheneOS, current feature/FAQ pages describing Android 16 baseline | Hardened Android sandbox and network permission; same app identity and execution-model constraints | Hardware-dependent verified boot and device security on its selected supported devices | Project-delivered OS updates; configurable auto-reboot can interrupt unattended sessions | **Good Android node baseline; poor generic server base.** Porting it to server hardware means taking responsibility for missing device support and security maintenance. [GRAPHENE-FEATURES] [GRAPHENE-FAQ] |
+| Bottlerocket, restricted-filesystem docs 1.25.x plus current project overview | Enforcing SELinux; container-oriented workloads. Host-UID mapping and Swarma egress need design | dm-verity root; complete physical boot/key assurance is a separate selected-platform check | Partition-based image updates and rollback; API-managed settings; reboot/drain | **Useful if Swarma deliberately adopts container orchestration.** Shell-less, package-manager-free host makes arbitrary native per-agent homes/helpers a larger adaptation. Avoid making a privileged admin container the normal agent boundary. [BOTTLEROCKET-FS] [BOTTLEROCKET] |
+| Flatcar, rolling docs | Container host with systemd; inspected SELinux guide says enforcement is not default. Set and verify explicit policy and egress | `/usr` dm-verity and signed update images documented. Full hardware-rooted chain/key keeper must be separately established | USR-A/USR-B updates; systemd integration; application health must be wired into rollback decisions | **Plausible immutable Linux host.** Lower ABI disruption, but immutability alone does not prove Android-equivalent MAC or boot attestation. Documentation includes older examples, so verify the chosen image's defaults. [FLATCAR-SEC] [FLATCAR-SELINUX] [FLATCAR-UPDATE] |
+| Fedora CoreOS, docs commit `1fec1acb5437220c559b8833e0ef1d643edd918b` | Enforcing SELinux; systemd services and container workloads; Swarma UID allocator/egress still custom | Do not infer full dm-verity/AVB-like runtime root verification or a KeyMint equivalent from OSTree alone; boot/key profile requires separate qualification | OSTree deployments, rpm-ostree, Zincati; reboot coordination and rollback; package extensions supported | **Leading host experiment candidate.** Keep a small native privileged boundary and qualified Linux userspace. Extensions/custom policy add maintenance; synchronize updates with Swarma locks and draining. [FCOS-SELINUX] [FCOS-UPDATE] [FCOS-EXT] |
+| ChromiumOS / minijail, verified-boot design and AOSP minijail Android 16 source | Minijail supplies containment/privilege-drop primitives; it is not a complete Swarma policy or OS | ChromiumOS supplies a verified-boot/recovery design; transplanting minijail alone supplies no boot chain or hardware key service | Full ChromiumOS product integration carries its image/recovery lifecycle | **Strong reference and reusable sandbox component.** Building a custom server product around the complete OS has device/image/toolchain work; using minijail on Linux is a smaller scope. [CHROMIUM-BOOT] [MINIJAIL] |
+| NixOS, rolling stable manual; systemd v257 mechanisms | Declarative users/services and systemd hardening; deliberate MAC/egress configuration required. Reconcile declarative users with a runtime-owned allocator | Reproducible/declarative configuration is not evidence of a hardware-rooted verified filesystem. Add and qualify a separate boot/key design | Versioned system generations and rollback; persistent application data needs independent migration/recovery | **Good option when declarative native services dominate.** Account reconciliation and packaging require care; service switching must respect runner lifetime and operator update locks. [NIXOS] [SYSTEMD] [SH] |
+| Talos Linux, v1.11 docs | API-managed immutable Kubernetes host; workload isolation and Swarma host-UID mapping need an explicit container/extension design | Documented Secure Boot signs bootloader and UKI; TPM2-backed encryption has a documented setup. This is not Swarma's deterministic signing keeper | Image-oriented lifecycle; operator API; Swarma session recovery still custom | **Good Kubernetes appliance, costly fit for current native-host model.** No normal host shell/SSH or package manager. Adopt only with an intentional Kubernetes workload redesign. [TALOS] [TALOS-ADMIN] [TALOS-BOOT] |
+
+A practical Linux reference profile would use separate persistent agent accounts and a generation ledger, narrow privileged launch operations, independent controller/runner service units, SELinux or an equivalently specified MAC policy, syscall restrictions, cgroups, per-UID egress plus a proxy, and a separately qualified hardware key service. systemd's `DynamicUser=` is **not** a shortcut to durable identity: v257 documents recycled UIDs and precautions for persistent files and passed descriptors. `StateDirectory=` can help storage handling but cannot replace Swarma generation checks. Boot verification and signed image updates should be explicit profile capabilities rather than assumptions. [UM] [CB] [SH] [SYSTEMD] [FCOS-SELINUX] [FLATCAR-SEC] [TALOS-BOOT]
+
+Two concrete Linux equivalents are nftables socket-UID matching (`meta skuid`) and TPM-backed signing through a dedicated keeper using TPM operations. They carry the packet-identity and hardware-operation parts of the design; they do not supply destination authorization, grant checks, or a portable deterministic Ed25519 hierarchy. The TPM's algorithms, key creation/import policy, authorization and attestation would need separate qualification. Thus the key-hierarchy tradeoff is not unique to Android, while the existing Linux launcher/toolchain path avoids Android-specific packaging work. [NFT] [TPM-SIGN] [B04] [UM]
+
+The assurance label should therefore be a measured capability set: caller identity/generation binding, cross-agent isolation, enforced egress, non-exporting key operations, authenticated boot/update path, controller-restart survival, and reboot recovery. Unsupported capabilities should be reported individually for Linux, macOS and Android. This research provides no basis for declaring every Linux/macOS deployment less fault-tolerant simply because Android has stronger defaults in some security areas. Security containment, update recoverability and service availability are different properties. [ARCH] [SH] [SANDBOX] [LMKD] [AVB] [SYSTEMD]
+
+## Android as an agent node
+
+**Recommended role:** a bounded, resumable capability provider with an outbound authenticated connection to the main host. Good first uses are an operator approval/key-signing client, a phone-local task or model adapter, and permission-scoped access to device capabilities. Keep authority in explicit Swarma grants and keep the main board/session recovery on the host. The phone is allowed to be asleep, offline, locked or terminated; requests need expiry, idempotency, and durable acknowledgement. This proposal follows the remote/bridge contracts and Android lifecycle constraints. [B08] [B11] [KEYSTORE] [DOZE] [FGS]
+
+| Node design | What it would take / useful boundary |
+| --- | --- |
+| One trusted Android bridge app with bounded subordinate jobs | Smallest initial client. Internal jobs share the app's ambient UID authority unless moved to isolated services. Report that limitation; do not advertise one-UID-per-agent isolation. Use explicit Binder interfaces, caller checks and no direct host-control tokens in jobs. [SANDBOX] [BINDER] [ISOLATED] |
+| Separate APK identity for each durable agent or capability | Real app UID separation with per-app storage/permissions. Needs signed package provisioning, lifecycle management and generation binding across removal/reinstallation. Better for a small fixed set of capabilities than high-churn arbitrary worker creation. [APPIDS] [SANDBOX] [UM] |
+| Isolated process service for short untrusted computation | Reduced ambient authority; expose only narrow brokered operations. It does not supply a durable home, arbitrary network permission, or restart-surviving session by itself. [ISOLATED] [SH] |
+| Native AOSP agent services on a managed device | Supports deeper launcher/network integration, at the distribution-maintenance cost described above. This is a separate product scope from installing an app on a user's existing device. [INIT] [SELINUX] [ASB] |
+| Linux guest or Microdroid payload | Additional isolation option on supported systems. Microdroid is Android/Bionic-based, not an off-the-shelf glibc workstation. A general Linux guest requires explicit host/guest authority and network mapping. Do not infer availability on every Android device or physical key assurance from a VM test. [MICRODROID] [AVF] [ATTEST] |
+
+For the remote-worker direction, an Android device can initiate the carrier/SSH connection and expose a constrained adapter without hosting project tools locally. The full “donor runs an unchanged vendor harness” role remains contingent on the binary probe below. Keep signing, encryption and SSH identities separate as the remote-workplace chapter requires, and never put project carrier credentials into the donor agent. A disconnect or reboot must not silently replay a previously applied approval or control action. [B11] [B08]
+
+For a human approval client, authentication-bound keys are useful; for an unattended worker, requiring a fresh human unlock for every key use would defeat that role. Give those roles separate policies instead of claiming both unattended availability and human presence for the same operation. Preserve locked-boot protections on a hardened phone; do not root a working device merely to emulate the native-host architecture. [KEYSTORE] [FBE] [GRAPHENE-FAQ]
+
+## Verdict and cheap first experiment
+
+| Proposed use | Verdict | Main reason |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Default OS for all Swarma infrastructure now | **Do not select AOSP at this stage.** | Too many unqualified changes at the identity, harness, key and lifetime boundaries; no demonstrated availability advantage. Hardened Linux aligns more closely with the current specs. [UM] [SH] [AID] [KEYMINT] [CLAUDE] [FCOS-EXT] |
+| Dedicated AOSP appliance later | **Conditional option.** | Revisit only with selected hardware, a demonstrated harness path, budget for platform/security maintenance, and explicit decisions on identity/key contracts. [BUILD] [ASB] [ATTEST] |
+| Android agent/capability node | **Yes, for bounded and resumable services.** | Useful app isolation, hardware key APIs and device integration; design around mobile lifecycle and honest per-agent assurance. [SANDBOX] [KEYSTORE] [DOZE] |
+| Android as a reference design | **Yes.** | Default-deny MAC, small privileged services and authenticated image updates are valuable patterns for the Linux host profile. [SELINUX] [INIT] [AVB] |
+
+**First experiment — proposed only, not executed:** test the earliest expensive assumption: that the exact vendor harness binaries and essential subprocess/terminal behavior can work on Android without disabling the security model. Failure would strongly weaken the AOSP-host case before we invest in an allocator, custom KeyMint integration or a full distribution. A successful probe would still not qualify the whole architecture. [CLAUDE] [CODEX] [APP-EXEC] [SH]
+
+1. Use one disposable **hosted Linux CI worker** with working KVM and a prepared Cuttlefish host environment. Pin a prebuilt Android 16 Cuttlefish image and matching host package by build ID and digest. AOSP explicitly requires the matching build; its host setup may require kernel modules and a reboot. If the chosen hosted runner cannot satisfy that, record an environment failure and use one disposable hosted VM with those capabilities. Never fall back to a working machine. **Proposed spend bound: one worker, two hours, no full AOSP build.** This is a budget proposal, not a measured runtime or cloud price. [CUTTLEFISH] [BUILD]
+2. Pin the exact Claude Code and Codex artifacts and inspect their ELF interpreter/dependencies. First run bounded startup/help and Codex app-server initialization probes under the guest's unprivileged shell, without accounts or real credentials. Record image/build fingerprint, ABI, SELinux state/context, binary hashes, command, exit code and denial/crash evidence. This lane answers only basic compatibility; the shell UID is not the proposed production daemon or app domain. [CLAUDE] [CODEX] [BIONIC] [SH]
+3. For a node-relevant check, package a tiny Android-targeted PTY/IPC probe in an APK targeting API 36 and test it under the actual app sandbox. Where redistribution/packaging is available through the chosen vendor distribution route, test the harness in that same intended execution context; otherwise mark that cell unknown. Test a controlled child process with output, resize, interrupt and exit. Attempt execution of a newly written app-data binary as an expected rejection. Do not use permissive SELinux, broad root access or a legacy target-SDK workaround to turn the rejection green. This distinguishes an ABI success from the ordinary APK's build-and-run limitation. [PTY] [APP-EXEC] [SANDBOX]
+4. Publish a small result matrix, with **passed/attempted** counts separately for binary startup, app-server protocol, PTY behavior, and negative policy probes. Preserve real nonzero statuses for expected rejections. Missing execution, absent credentials, or unsupported packaging stays “not tested,” not a pass. No API/model call is needed for the initial compatibility gate; authenticated model work and credential renewal remain untested. [SH] [CB]
+
+The immediate exit is one of: **incompatible in the tested context**, **basic compatibility only**, or **environment prevented the probe**. Do not turn the last outcome into evidence against Android. If only a Linux guest works, evaluate it as a Linux guest architecture rather than silently crediting native Android per-agent guarantees. [MICRODROID] [SH]
+
+The next production slice, only if the probe warrants it, is a small custom-image integration of the **real** user-manager/launcher → authenticated broker → independently supervised runner → PTY/app-server path, with two agent identities. It must prove wrong-UID and stale-generation rejection, deny-before-exec egress, controller restart with the same runner still alive, old-epoch input rejection, explicit-stop descendant cleanup, and closed fences after reboot. Narrow an allowed network profile and show the newly forbidden destination fails; deleting a rule is not a sufficient bound test. This is the first vertical implementation gate, not another open-ended research phase. Physical hardware key/boot attestation would be a subsequent separate gate. [UM] [CB] [SH] [NETD] [INIT-REAP] [ATTEST]
+
+**Unresolved by design:** exact harness release behavior on Android; hardware-backed keeper support on a selected server; permissible UID/GID allocation and netd integration for that image; controller-independent native runner supervision; and the operational cost of carrying the platform. None is represented here as established. The recommendation can change if the hosted probe and that vertical slice produce evidence strong enough to justify the added maintenance.
